-- allium: 3
-- read-model-processor-v2.allium
--
-- Distilled from components/read-model-processor-v2 (grain).
--
-- Keyed execution: independent per-instance state and compact results, atomic
-- index maintenance, indexed pagination/search, and composed read queries are
-- specified by KeyedFoldDefinition, KeyedFoldMaterialization, KeyedFoldReads and
-- ComposedReadQuery, exposed through KeyedFoldBoundary. They do not use legacy
-- L1/TTL caches, speculative routing or deferred write-back. The original
-- projection entities, rules and invariants below describe the legacy project API.
--
-- Scope: The read side of grain's CQRS model. A read model is a pure reducer
--        `(state, event) -> state` that folds a tenant's event stream into a
--        queryable state map. This processor turns that reducer plus a query
--        into a current projection, and makes repeated reads cheap by caching
--        the folded state and resuming from a watermark rather than re-folding
--        from the beginning. This spec captures the behavioural guarantees the
--        engine promises: projection correctness and determinism, incremental
--        catch-up from a stored watermark, cache transparency across tiers,
--        tenant and scope isolation, partition correctness (including
--        cross-partition moves), and serialization round-trip.
--
-- Includes: deterministic projection as a fold over the matching event stream;
--           a stored (state, watermark) cache advanced incrementally as new
--           events arrive; a fast in-process tier (L1) layered over a durable
--           tier (L2) that must agree with a from-scratch projection; TTL-gated
--           freshness for L1; per-tenant and per-scope cache isolation; size-
--           transparent storage (monolithic vs segmented) and partitioned
--           projection with single-partition reads and automatic cross-partition
--           move detection; the registry of named read models; reducer purity
--           and resilience of the speculative relevance probe.
--
-- Excludes (implementation, governed elsewhere):
--   - the event store itself (querying, ordering, watermarks as event ids):
--     this spec demands the EventStream contract and owns only how a read model
--     consumes it
--   - the durable cache backend (LMDB key/value store) and its keying scheme:
--     only the (state, watermark) round-trip and tier-agreement guarantees are
--     specified, not the byte layout or key bytes
--   - serialization / wire format (Fressian) — only the round-trip guarantee
--   - the precise eviction policy (LRU by entry count), segment count (64),
--     hash-segmentation thresholds and write-back batching counts: these are
--     performance tunings recorded as config, not behavioural promises
--   - CloudWatch / mulog metrics and the cache-tier trace labels
--   - threads, atoms, transients and other in-process mechanics
--
-- Conformance properties RM1..RM11 correspond to the interface test suite
-- (interface_test.clj). Where each is captured:
--   RM1  fold correctness        -> contract Projection.FoldOverMatchingEvents
--   RM2  determinism             -> contract Projection.Deterministic
--   RM3  incremental catch-up    -> rule ProjectReadModel + Cache.watermark
--   RM4  no reprocessing         -> contract Projection.NoReprocessing
--   RM5  cache transparency      -> contract Cache.TierAgreement
--   RM6  L1 TTL freshness        -> rule ProjectReadModel + invariant FreshWhenZeroTtl
--   RM7  tenant isolation        -> invariant CachePerTenant + surface guarantee
--   RM8  scope isolation         -> invariant CachePerScope
--   RM9  size transparency       -> contract Cache.SizeTransparent
--   RM10 partition correctness   -> contract Partitioning.* + invariant EntityInOnePartition
--   RM11 serialization roundtrip -> contract StateCodec.Roundtrip

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A domain tenant. Tenants are governed by the event store / control plane, not
-- here; every projection is scoped to exactly one tenant and the processor only
-- ever folds and caches that tenant's events.
external entity Tenant {}

-- An immutable fact in a tenant's event stream, supplied by the event store. A
-- read model subscribes to a subset of event types and folds the matching ones.
-- Only the attributes a reducer and the engine actually observe are modelled.
external entity Event {
    tenant: Tenant
    -- The event's position in the tenant's total order. The cache stores the id
    -- of the last folded event as its watermark, so the next read resumes
    -- strictly after it.
    id: EventId
    -- The domain meaning of the event; a read model declares which types it
    -- subscribes to.
    event_type: String

    -- An event is foldable by a read model when its type is one the read model
    -- subscribes to. The engine only ever folds foldable events.
    is_subscribed_by(rm): event_type in rm.event_types
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- An opaque event identifier supplied by the event store. The processor never
-- inspects its structure: it uses it only as a cursor ("after this id") that the
-- cache advances on each catch-up. Total ordering of ids within a tenant's stream
-- is a guarantee of the EventStream contract, not something this processor derives.
value EventId {
    value: String
}

-- Optional inert Malli data describing the shape of the accumulated projection.
-- Registration validates that it parses; projection execution does not validate
-- each intermediate or returned state against it.
value StateSchema {}

-- A description of which of a tenant's events a read model folds: the subscribed
-- event types, plus an optional scope that narrows the set further (a tag filter
-- or an explicit set of sub-queries). Two reads whose effective query differs
-- are cached independently.
value Query {
    -- The event types this read model subscribes to.
    types: Set<String>
    -- An optional narrowing of the stream (e.g. by entity tag). Absent means the
    -- whole subscribed stream for the tenant.
    scope: Scope?
}

-- A narrowing applied on top of a read model's subscribed types. Distinct scopes
-- produce distinct cache entries even for the same read model and tenant.
value Scope {
    -- An opaque description of the narrowing (tag set or override sub-queries).
    -- The engine does not interpret it beyond using it to key the cache and to
    -- constrain which events are folded.
    descriptor: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The event stream the processor reads. The processor demands these guarantees
-- from the event store; it does not provide them. Catch-up correctness depends
-- on the store returning, for a tenant, exactly the matching events strictly
-- after a given watermark, in ascending id order.
contract EventStream {
    -- All matching events for a tenant in ascending id order.
    read: (tenant: Tenant, query: Query) -> Sequence<Event>
    -- The matching events for a tenant strictly after a watermark, ascending.
    read_after: (tenant: Tenant, query: Query, watermark: EventId) -> Sequence<Event>

    @invariant TenantScopedAscending
        -- read and read_after return only the named tenant's events, ascending
        -- by event id (chronological). read_after returns exactly the events the
        -- corresponding read would, minus those up to and including the
        -- watermark — so concatenating successive catch-ups reconstructs the
        -- full stream with no gap and no overlap.
}

-- The reducer a read model is built from: a pure fold step. This is a property
-- of caller-supplied code, so it lives as a contract the processor demands
-- rather than as entity state.
contract Reducer {
    -- Fold one event into the accumulated state.
    step: (state: Any, event: Event) -> Any

    @invariant Pure
        -- RM2: step is pure and deterministic — no I/O, no clock, no mutable
        -- state outside its arguments. The same (state, event) always yields the
        -- same next state. The engine relies on this to cache and incrementally
        -- replay folds.

    @invariant ReceivesFullEvents
        -- RM1: step is called with the whole event (its id, type, timestamp and
        -- body), never a projection of it, so a reducer can branch on event type
        -- and read any field.

    @invariant NilStateTolerant
        -- The engine probes step speculatively against empty state to decide
        -- whether a not-yet-seen event is relevant to a partition. A reducer that
        -- assumes prior state may itself fail on such a probe; the engine treats
        -- a failed probe as "not relevant" and must not let it abort the read.
        -- (Captured as @guarantee ProbeIsResilient on the projection surface.)
}

-- The state serialization guarantee for the durable tier. Folded state survives
-- a store-and-read cycle unchanged. The encoding is an implementation choice;
-- only the round-trip identity is promised.
contract StateCodec {
    serialize: (state: Any) -> ByteArray
    deserialize: (bytes: ByteArray) -> Any

    @invariant Roundtrip
        -- RM11: deserialize(serialize(state)) reproduces the state exactly,
        -- including rich value types a reducer may place in it (e.g. timestamps),
        -- with no loss. A projection served from the durable tier equals the same
        -- projection held in memory.
}

-- The projection guarantee: what the engine computes, independent of caching.
contract Projection {
    @invariant FoldOverMatchingEvents
        -- RM1: the projection for a (read model, tenant, scope) is the reducer
        -- folded, in event-id order, over exactly the tenant's events that match
        -- the read model's query — starting from empty state. Events that do not
        -- match are never folded.

    @invariant Deterministic
        -- RM2: the projection depends only on the matching event stream and the
        -- reducer. The same events and reducer always yield the same state,
        -- regardless of how many intermediate reads occurred or which tier
        -- served them.

    @invariant NoReprocessing
        -- RM4: across successive reads, each event is folded at most once. A read
        -- that finds new events folds only those past the stored watermark onto
        -- the cached state; a read that finds none folds nothing. The cumulative
        -- result equals a from-scratch fold over all events seen so far.
}

-- The cache guarantee: the two tiers are an optimisation, never a source of
-- truth that can disagree with the projection.
contract Cache {
    @invariant TierAgreement
        -- RM5: whatever tier serves a read — the in-process tier, the durable
        -- tier, or a from-scratch projection on a cold cache — the returned state
        -- is the projection defined by contract Projection. Caches change cost
        -- and latency, never the answer.

    @invariant SizeTransparent
        -- RM9: state is stored monolithically when small and split into a fixed
        -- set of hash segments when large, migrating between the two as it grows.
        -- The choice and the segment boundaries are invisible to a reader: the
        -- reassembled state equals the projection either way, and on an
        -- incremental update only the segments that actually changed are
        -- rewritten.
}

-- The partitioning guarantee for read models that shard their state by a
-- partition key derived from entity state.
contract Partitioning {
    -- The partition an entity belongs to, derived from its current state.
    partition_of: (entity: Any) -> PartitionKey
    -- The entity an event concerns, used to route the event to a partition.
    entity_of: (event: Event) -> EntityId

    @invariant FullEqualsMergedPartitions
        -- RM10: a full projection (no partition key) equals the merge of every
        -- partition's state. Reading one partition returns exactly that
        -- partition's slice of the full projection — no other partition's
        -- entities, none of its own missing.

    @invariant CrossPartitionMove
        -- RM10: when folding an event changes an entity's partition key, the
        -- entity leaves its old partition and appears in the new one, exactly
        -- once. A single-partition read can both shed an entity that moved out
        -- and adopt one that moved in, without a full reprojection — and a later
        -- read of either partition sees the move.

    @invariant PartitionIsolation
        -- RM10: appending events to one partition does not alter another. Each
        -- partition is cached and caught up independently from its own watermark.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- How long the in-process tier may serve a cached projection without
    -- re-checking the event store. Zero means always re-check, so reads are
    -- never stale; a positive window trades freshness for sub-millisecond reads
    -- during rapid interaction (pagination, filter changes). Defaulted per read
    -- model; the engine default is zero.
    l1_ttl: Duration = 0.seconds

    -- Soft cap on the number of distinct projections held in process; the least
    -- recently used are dropped past this point. A capacity knob, not a
    -- behavioural promise — a dropped entry is simply recomputed on next read.
    l1_max_entries: Integer = 10000

    -- A durable-tier write-back happens only when a single catch-up read folds
    -- at least this many new events, to amortise serialization cost. The count is
    -- per catch-up, not cumulative across reads: several reads each folding fewer
    -- than the threshold leave the durable copy untouched even though their total
    -- exceeds it. Below the threshold the in-process tier still reflects the new
    -- events; only the durable copy lags, and a cold read re-folds the remainder.
    write_back_threshold: Integer = 10
}

contract KeyedFoldDefinition {
    initial: () -> Any
    key: (event: Any) -> Any
    route: (event: Any) -> Any
    step: (state: Any, event: Any) -> Any
    result: (state: Any) -> Any
    include: (result: Any) -> Boolean

    @invariant IndependentPureFunctions
        -- Each model is independently defined. Initial state, routing, reduction,
        -- result calculation, and result-index extraction are deterministic.
        -- Reducers receive ordinary immutable detached state and their routed
        -- input (the original event for key-based definitions); result functions
        -- receive only that model instance's state.
        -- No callback requires transaction handles, storage mutations, another
        -- projection, request context, a clock, or external effects. The caller
        -- supplies functions honouring this obligation; runtime enforcement of
        -- arbitrary program purity is not promised.

    @invariant StableOwnership
        -- A keyed definition supplies exactly one of key or route. Key supplies
        -- one supported non-null stable owner and passes the original event.
        -- Route supplies a finite ordered sequence of two-element vectors
        -- [owner, input], determined solely from the original event. An empty
        -- sequence deliberately selects no state; nil, malformed pairs and null
        -- owners are errors. Several inputs for the same owner apply in their
        -- returned order, after all inputs from earlier stored events.
        -- Each owner's reduction must have all the state it needs inside that
        -- instance. The processor never infers ownership by probing a reducer
        -- with empty state, consulting another projection, or using live context.

    @invariant OriginalEventProgress
        -- Routed inputs are transient reducer arguments, never appended events.
        -- Event selection, ordering and progress use the original stored event.
        -- All inputs from one event are processed within one batch transaction;
        -- their states, results, indexes and progress commit together or not at
        -- all. Even an event with no inputs advances progress on success.

    @invariant LocalCompletion
        -- The result is a pure function of accumulated state, not an independently
        -- advancing projection. Omitting the function means identity. Completion
        -- is nondestructive: later events continue from accumulated state, not
        -- from the compact result. It may be evaluated after a batch rather than
        -- after every event without changing the observable answer.

    @invariant ExplicitResultMembership
        -- An optional pure inclusion predicate decides whether a completion is
        -- published as a readable result. It defaults to inclusion, including
        -- nil. Excluded keys retain accumulated state so later events can make
        -- them eligible. Inclusion changes atomically add or remove the result,
        -- every index entry and the counted membership with state and progress.
        -- Exclusion does not evaluate index extractors. Predicate failure rolls
        -- back the batch. Changing this predicate requires a new model version.

    @invariant EvolutionAndCompatibility
        -- Changes to routing, reduction, result calculation, initial state,
        -- schemas, or indexes require a new model version and fresh fold.
        -- Existing declarations without keyed execution retain their previous
        -- projection behaviour and return shapes. Keyed and legacy identities
        -- cannot accidentally read one another's materializations.

    @invariant EffectiveDeclarationOptions
        -- Unknown options and options belonging only to the other execution
        -- model are rejected at registration, with the offending keys reported.
        -- Diagnostics name the model, distinguish unknown from inapplicable
        -- options, list supported options and explain how to correct the declaration.
        -- A typo cannot silently discard inclusion, result or index behavior.
        -- Filtered paging support is named filtered-indexes. No alternate
        -- declaration spelling is accepted. Updating application declarations
        -- to this name preserves materialization identity without replay.
}

contract KeyedFoldMaterialization {
    catch_up: (context: Any, model: Any) -> Any

    @invariant FoldEquivalence
        -- At committed progress H, each instance state equals reduction from the
        -- declared initial state over exactly the inputs routed from subscribed
        -- tenant events through H, preserving event and input order. Its result equals
        -- result(state). Batch boundaries, prior read choices, restart, and
        -- interleaving other keys cannot change that answer.

    @invariant ContextSuppliesTenancy
        -- Tenant identity comes from trusted caller context. The configured
        -- cache belongs to its event store; no additional history identifier
        -- is supplied by the application.
        -- State, results, indexes, progress, and read positions are isolated by
        -- tenant, model, and version within that cache; instances are distinguished
        -- by key. Definitions contain no tenant-routing or cross-tenant selector.
        -- A single-tenant deployment uses the same definitions and contracts.

    @invariant StableMaterializationIdentity
        -- Internal addressing choices are invisible to callers. Concurrent first
        -- use, later reads, and reopening the configured cache resolve the same
        -- tenant, model, and version consistently; distinct scopes never alias.
        -- A read session keeps one consistent resolution for its lifetime.
        -- Failed or uncertain identity initialization cannot expose another
        -- scope's materialization or make partially published results readable.
        -- Inconsistent durable identity information encountered while resolving
        -- an address fails explicitly before serving or replacing data through
        -- that address; it is not guessed or reassigned.

    @invariant CoordinatedPublication
        -- A successful batch publishes all its affected accumulated states,
        -- results, index changes, counts, and model progress together. Every
        -- event covered by advanced progress has its complete effects committed.
        -- Result calculation and indexing have no independent refresh checkpoint.

    @invariant FailureAndRestart
        -- Failure in routing, reduction, completion, validation, encoding, or
        -- indexing publishes none of that batch. Earlier successful batches
        -- remain committed. Recovery resumes from durable progress and agrees
        -- with a clean replay. An uncertain commit outcome must be resolved from
        -- persisted progress before replaying, not guessed from in-memory state.

    @invariant ConcurrentProgress
        -- Concurrent readers and catch-up attempts cannot double-apply committed
        -- events, regress progress, or overwrite newer state with an older fold.
        -- Application reducers do not coordinate writers themselves.

    @invariant FiniteFreshness
        -- A read captures a finite source head and catches its model up through
        -- that head before serving fresh data. Newer committed state may be
        -- observed; later events cannot cause an unbounded chase. A failed
        -- catch-up is an error rather than a successful stale fallback.
        -- Capturing the head from a built-in store reads its committed identity
        -- directly, without decoding the latest transaction marker or payload.
        -- A large source batch therefore does not inflate every later freshness
        -- check. Stores without direct-head support retain bounded reverse reads.

    @invariant EqualResultsPreserveState
        -- If new state has an equal result, changed state and progress still
        -- commit. Result/index replacement may be omitted. A result equality
        -- check must never discard state required to interpret the next event.

    @invariant DisposableMaterialization
        -- Incompatible stored formats are not migrated. A new storage-format
        -- identity materializes through normal event folding; old derived data
        -- is unused and may be discarded independently of the event history.
        -- Changing or restoring a derived representation never rewrites source
        -- events or rewinds durable processor checkpoints. Before serving a
        -- fresh read, a rebuilt or restored representation satisfies the same
        -- captured-head freshness contract as an ordinary materialization.

    @invariant TimeDoesNotMutateMaterialization
        -- Advancing the query date alone does not change accumulated state,
        -- results, indexes, or model progress. Time-dependent answers evaluate
        -- date-independent results with an explicit query date. Ordinary event
        -- catch-up remains allowed when a date-changing query also sees events.
}

contract KeyedFoldReads {
    state: (context: Any, model: Any, key: Any, not_found: Any) -> Any
    value: (context: Any, model: Any, key: Any, not_found: Any) -> Any
    values: (context: Any, model: Any, selection: Any) -> Any
    page: (context: Any, model: Any, selection: Any) -> Any
    index_groups: (context: Any, model: Any, index: Any) -> Any
    index_keys: (context: Any, model: Any, selection: Any) -> Any
    index_rank: (context: Any, model: Any, selection: Any, key: Any) -> Any
    search_keys: (context: Any, model: Any, selection: Any) -> Any
    key_page: (context: Any, model: Any, keys: Any, selection: Any) -> Any
    filtered_key_page: (context: Any, model: Any, keys: Any, selection: Any) -> Any
    joined_page: (context: Any, membership_model: Any, ordering_model: Any, selection: Any) -> Any

    @invariant RankedJoinWithMissingValues
        -- An identity join may take membership from one model and stored ordering
        -- from another within one read session. Only included membership-model
        -- instances qualify. Optional candidate identities restrict that set.
        -- Missing or excluded ordering results take the caller's explicit default
        -- sort value, interleaved with real equal-valued results by ascending
        -- string identity in both directions. No default result is persisted.
        -- Exact totals, clamping and page keys match full join-and-sort semantics.
        -- The ordering index must opt into rank membership. Query-time computed
        -- sorting is unsupported. Selection decodes no result or state payloads.
        -- Without candidates, selection traverses membership identities. With
        -- candidates, it probes their membership metadata. Defaulted identities
        -- may be sorted for tie ordering; all work and allocation remain measured.
        -- This does not imply constant-time joins or page-sized total metadata work.

    @invariant ExperimentalFilteredRanks
        -- A model may opt selected stored indexes into experimental membership
        -- summaries. Enabling or changing this selection requires a new model
        -- version. Summaries publish atomically with ordinary index changes.
        -- Filtered-key paging is the public operation for this capability.
        -- Ordinary indexes already support rank navigation;
        -- the optional capability adds efficient selection within a subset.
        -- Ranked matching-key pages have the same membership, order, counts,
        -- clamping and ties as matching-key pages with a stored sort order.
        -- Duplicate, excluded and absent keys do not inflate counts. Candidate
        -- identities are resolved in the target model; model-local numeric
        -- identities are never interchangeable across independent models.
        -- Query-time sort callbacks and indexes not opted in are rejected.
        -- Navigation counts matching members in stored spans and skips spans
        -- before the requested offset, without sorting candidate values or
        -- decoding complete result/state payloads. Resolving candidates and
        -- intersecting summaries may still require population-proportional work.
        -- The existing matching-key page remains available for comparison.

    @invariant SearchPublication
        -- Named searchable text is extracted purely from each included result.
        -- Text changes, exclusion and re-entry update exact substring matches
        -- in the same transaction as results and progress. Failure leaves none
        -- of the batch visible. Restart uses persisted search entries, with no
        -- second checkpoint, external search service or application cache writes.
        -- Changing text extraction requires a new model version and fresh fold.

    @invariant ExactTextMatching
        -- Matching uses Locale-independent lowercasing and Unicode code points.
        -- Contains queries require at least three code points. Explicit prefix
        -- and suffix queries support nonempty fragments for query-time composition.
        -- Repeated grams, positions and boundaries are significant: results are
        -- exact literal matches, not approximate candidates or token matches.
        -- Search returns detached instance identities without loading result or
        -- accumulated-state payloads. Cache, tenant, model and version isolation
        -- and prepared read-session lifetime are the same as other keyed reads.

    @invariant MatchingKeyPages
        -- A query may supply candidate identities and order their existing result
        -- membership using stored compact sort values before selecting a page.
        -- Duplicate identities count once and excluded or absent identities do
        -- not count. Counts, clamping and ascending string-key ties are exact.
        -- Optional query-time sort calculation receives identity and the chosen
        -- stored sort value, without loading complete results or changing them.
        -- Matching-key sorting may visit all candidates; this is not filtered
        -- rank seeking. Only the application-selected rows need full hydration.
        -- Page selection retains sort records only for the requested prefix
        -- (page number times page size), or fewer when fewer candidates qualify.
        -- It still examines every candidate needed for exact membership and
        -- totals. Beyond-end requests clamp to the real last page, including
        -- requests whose nominal offset exceeds machine integer bounds.

    @invariant StateAndValueAreDistinct
        -- State reads return accumulated state; value, result traversal, and
        -- result pages return completion values with their instance identities.
        -- Returned values are detached and remain usable after reads complete.
        -- Callers never receive a mutable storage handle as projection state.

    @invariant AbsenceIsExplicit
        -- Reading a never-seen key returns not_found and does not instantiate
        -- state or add a row to traversal. Stored nil is a present value, distinct
        -- from absence when the caller supplies a distinct not_found marker.
        -- Empty accumulated state does not implicitly delete an instance.
        -- Explicitly excluded results return not_found and do not appear in
        -- traversal or counts; their accumulated state remains readable.
        -- Domain membership can exclude a present result; storage presence
        -- alone does not establish inclusion in a query's root model.

    @invariant ResultReadIsolation
        -- When no catch-up is needed, result-only reads and traversals do not
        -- load accumulated-state payloads. Catch-up loads the states of affected
        -- keys, not the whole model population. One large key may remain costly;
        -- this contract does not bound the size of an application's local state.

    @invariant IndexAgreement
        -- Index membership and ordering are calculated only from committed
        -- results. Replacing a result removes obsolete index positions. A page's
        -- entries, order, count, and progress agree within one committed snapshot.
        -- Equal sort values use the string form of the instance key ascending
        -- as the final tie-breaker in both sort directions.
        -- Oversized storage keys fail with an identifiable key-too-large error
        -- before committing the affected batch; values are never truncated.

    @invariant RepresentationPreservesAdmissionAndOrder
        -- A storage representation change does not newly reject a previously
        -- accepted valid input as an oversized key merely because its encoding
        -- differs. It does not narrow an existing accepted string domain.
        -- String ordering retains UTF-16 code-unit comparison, including NUL,
        -- supplementary pairs, and unpaired surrogates. Tuple components keep
        -- their ordinary types and boundaries in either direction.
        -- Distinct supported identities with the same string form remain
        -- distinct and retain their deterministic typed ordering. Descending
        -- sort values do not reverse ascending identity ties.
        -- Exact counts, presence, and insertion ranks remain unchanged for
        -- absent identities, including a requested identity group different
        -- from the absent key's string form and an empty or non-string group.

    @invariant TraversalAndLifetime
        -- Traversal can be consumed without materializing the full result set,
        -- honours early termination, and releases read resources on completion
        -- or failure. Its valid consumption lifetime is explicit. Detached
        -- accumulated state or result values do not expire with the traversal.

    @invariant CompositionRemainsAQuery
        -- Queries can combine independent models' results in a scoped read
        -- session sharing one cache and event store. Catch-up runs once before
        -- the session's reads, without each addressed read starting
        -- another replay. This creates no shared model definition, version, or
        -- checkpoint and no persisted cross-model dependency graph. No historical
        -- atomic snapshot across independent event logs is implied.

    @invariant SelectionBeforePaging
        -- Application membership, search, computed ordering, and missing-value
        -- rules apply to the complete candidate population before pagination.
        -- A direct source index may be used only when it preserves these rules.
        -- Query composition may scan compact results. Exact counts, arbitrary
        -- substring matches and computed sorts have no constant-time
        -- guarantee and may require work proportional to the candidate population.

    @invariant RankedSourcePages
        -- A direct index page uses maintained ranks to seek its offset and to
        -- count an equality or tuple-prefix selection, rather than enumerating all preceding or
        -- matching members. Only returned result payloads are decoded. Counts,
        -- clamping, ascending key ties and read-session isolation are unchanged.
        -- Rank metadata commits with index membership in the fold transaction.
        -- Navigation has probabilistic bounds; this promises neither constant
        -- time nor page-sized work for arbitrary cross-model composition.

    @invariant TuplePrefixSelection
        -- Result traversal and direct pages may select vector-valued index
        -- entries by their complete leading components. A prefix [true] selects
        -- vectors beginning with true; it does not select scalar true. Components
        -- retain their ordinary types and exact boundaries: a string component
        -- is an equality match, not a text-prefix search, and a nested vector
        -- component must match in full. The empty prefix selects all vectors.
        -- Matching entries retain the index's ordinary ascending or descending
        -- order and ascending identity ties. Exact totals, empty pages, clamping,
        -- updates and read-session isolation follow ordinary indexed pages.
        -- Prefixes must be vectors and cannot be combined with equality selection.
        -- Selection reuses existing ordered index membership and ranks, without
        -- scanning candidate sort metadata or introducing a second materialization.

    @invariant TuplePrefixUnionPages
        -- A page may select a union of complete-component tuple prefixes from
        -- one stored index. Results use that index's global order, independent
        -- of supplied prefix order. Duplicate and overlapping prefixes include
        -- each instance only once; an empty collection selects no instances.
        -- Exact total, page clamping, both directions and ascending identity
        -- ties have the same semantics as single-prefix paging. A page can cross
        -- several selected ranges without loading the intervening results.
        -- Stored rank bounds count and skip whole ranges. Work scales with
        -- requested ranges and page size, not with the selected population or
        -- skipped pages. Only returned results are decoded. The selection uses
        -- one tenant-scoped prepared snapshot and reflects canonical updates.
        -- Each prefix must be a vector; union selection cannot be combined with
        -- single-prefix or equality selection. No additional index or persisted
        -- union state is required.

    @invariant ReadOnlyIndexComposition
        -- Queries may enumerate distinct sort values and their exact counts,
        -- or read counts for specified values without enumerating other groups,
        -- seek a bounded slice of instance identities within an equality group,
        -- and obtain a key's insertion rank and presence within that group.
        -- A slice starting at offset zero seeks the group's stored key prefix
        -- directly, without rank navigation or reading other groups. Nonzero
        -- offsets retain rank navigation so skipped members are not scanned.
        -- Queries may also read selected forward index values for specified
        -- identities, or reduce them across the model. Each entry contains its
        -- identity and only the requested named index values. Absent identities
        -- are omitted; a stored nil remains a present index value. Enumeration
        -- is reducible and honours early termination.
        -- Temporary duplicate tracking and field assembly are local to each
        -- reduction; they need not rebuild immutable intermediates per item.
        -- Returned metadata maps remain detached immutable values, and repeating
        -- a reduction starts with fresh duplicate tracking.
        -- This reads compact index metadata, never result or reducer-state
        -- payloads, and does not claim
        -- page-sized work when a calculation examines every candidate.
        -- Computed ordering may consume several named forward index values
        -- in one pass over candidate identities or all included identities.
        -- Each candidate's sort expression is evaluated once; selection and
        -- stable identity ties do not require decoding full model results.
        -- A query may filter candidates using the same named index values
        -- before ordering. The predicate runs once per distinct present
        -- candidate; only accepted candidates are ordered and counted.
        -- Filtering and ordering share one metadata read per candidate.
        -- Reported metadata reads include rejected candidates. Empty matches
        -- retain the ordinary empty-page and clamping semantics.
        -- Identity slices and rank probes do not load result or state payloads.
        -- Group enumeration reads one maintained count per distinct value,
        -- without loading representative results. Counts change in the
        -- same transaction as membership; empty groups disappear.
        -- All operations respect the same read-session snapshot and identity.
        -- Missing groups have zero count; slices beyond their end are empty.
        -- These reads create no stored join, mutation API or refresh lifecycle.
}

contract ComposedReadQuery {
    execute: (context: Any, definition: Any, request: Any) -> Any

    @invariant ApplicationSuppliesMeaning
        -- A definition names one root keyed model, to-one keyed relationships,
        -- searchable result fields, and allowed orderings, including pure
        -- calculations with explicit dependencies. The caller supplies
        -- request parameters, including a date when calculations require it.
        -- Existing canonical reducers, keyed models and compact results remain
        -- independently defined. Authorization and rendering remain in the application.
        -- Applications supply no rank probes, candidate-set mutation, merge
        -- algorithms, read-session coordination, or execution-path selection.

    @invariant QueryNotProjection
        -- The definition does not create a persisted joined result or a new
        -- event-consuming reducer. Root inclusion determines one row per root
        -- key. Relationships do not multiply rows or silently remove a root.
        -- Stored indexes remain derived from independently owned results;
        -- application code does not maintain or refresh them.

    @invariant ExplicitDependencies
        -- Related keys refer to a root key or previously bound result field.
        -- The relationship setting :key specifies the lookup ID. The expression
        -- [:id alias] obtains a binding's instance identity; :key is not also
        -- an expression operator. Field references remain :alias/field.
        -- Calculated orderings declare every value and request parameter they use.
        -- Callbacks receive these values, never storage access or request context.
        -- Definition cycles and unresolved references are rejected.
        -- A function dependency declaration does not establish that the function
        -- is indexable, monotonic, or independent of request parameters.

    @invariant IndexCorrespondence
        -- Pure order-key and search-text extractor helpers declare which result
        -- fields they extract and their normalization. The planner matches that
        -- information structurally; it never guesses arbitrary function meaning.
        -- Existing opaque extractors remain usable through the low-level API.
        -- A related model's ordering can serve a root page directly only when
        -- stored exact identity membership proves their included keys equal.
        -- Otherwise preserve root membership with an exact selection strategy.
        -- Membership summaries commit with each independent fold's results and
        -- progress. They contain identities, not cross-model business values.

    @invariant MinimalDeclaration
        -- The data surface has four sections: from, related, search
        -- and orders. From binds exactly one alias to a root model;
        -- related binds aliases to named models and explicit lookup keys.
        -- There is no fields section or output-field mapping. Search references
        -- bound result fields directly; orders maps public names to ordered
        -- lists of result-field references or pure calls with explicit arguments.
        -- The request chooses order, direction,
        -- page, limit, search text and explicit calculation parameters.
        -- This initial surface fixes search to space-separated, case-insensitive
        -- literal text with the existing three-code-point minimum. Text ordering
        -- is case-insensitive with missing text treated as empty; final root-ID
        -- ties remain case-sensitive ascending strings. These are documented
        -- semantics, not application knobs. The caller supplies its default order
        -- when normalizing the page request; map iteration never chooses defaults.

    @invariant BoundResults
        -- Each selected row returns the root and related compact results under
        -- their declared aliases. A reserved key field carries root identity;
        -- aliases may not use that reserved name. Preserve identity for ties.
        -- Return exact pagination metadata alongside these rows. Grain loads
        -- declared relationships for selected rows even when those relationships
        -- did not participate in search or ordering.
        -- Application views may consume these results directly or use existing
        -- pure business functions to shape a display row. Display calculations
        -- run after page selection; they are not another query declaration layer.

    @invariant RelationshipIntegrity
        -- Relationships are required unless a single missing policy explicitly
        -- supplies a default value, including nil for an optional relationship.
        -- Syntax is missing: error (also the default), or missing:
        -- [default, value]. Separate required and optional flags do not exist.
        -- Defaults apply only to an absent result, not a stored nil or a failing
        -- validator. Pure validation receives the lookup key and resolved value,
        -- including any explicitly defaulted value. Its return value is ignored;
        -- throwing reports a domain-integrity error, never a fallback lookup.
        -- Missing required data or domain-validation failure is an explicit
        -- query error. It must not silently drop a row or substitute history.
        -- Validators run on accessed results, including related results read
        -- to resolve ordering groups. They do not audit unaccessed results.
        -- Reusing a result obtained during page selection does not bypass its
        -- relationship validator. Selected results satisfy validation regardless
        -- of which index or ordering supplied them.

    @invariant QuerySemantics
        -- Search and ordering apply before page selection. Counts are exact,
        -- numbered pages clamp, and final ties use ascending string root ID in
        -- either direction. Allowed sorts and text normalization are explicit.
        -- Search is case-insensitive literal substring matching of the declared
        -- fields separated by spaces, including matches crossing field boundaries.
        -- Nonempty searches shorter than
        -- three Unicode code points are unsupported without a population query.
        -- A related result's rename changes the next query without rewriting roots.

    @invariant ExecutionOwnership
        -- Grain resolves dependencies, prepares reads, selects an execution
        -- strategy, combines memberships and searches, selects the page and
        -- composes its rows. Application definitions name no storage ranks,
        -- postings, bitmap operations, or strategy-specific callbacks.
        -- Native indexed paths retain existing read and page-depth bounds.
        -- Applications calculate display-only values for selected rows. Grain
        -- evaluates declared calculations when ordering requires them. A calculated sort
        -- may require values for all matches; the declaration cannot make an
        -- arbitrary pure calculation efficiently orderable by itself.

    @invariant InitialExecutionLimits
        -- Indexed text composition supports one contiguous root text segment,
        -- optionally followed by one related text segment reached through an
        -- exact equality index on the root or a same-key related model. Text
        -- extractors must match the fields. Grain handles the separator between
        -- segments. One text index serves both root-only and related searches;
        -- applications need not declare a separator suffix. Existing empty or
        -- space-suffixed extractors remain usable without a version change.
        -- A non-nil related default cannot serve as indexed search text: it is
        -- not present in the related model's index. Reject that search shape.
        -- Unsupported search shapes and missing field-order index support fail
        -- during definition validation, before event catch-up or query reads.
        -- This validation also applies when obtaining subscription dependencies.
        -- Definition errors identify the offending order or search relationship,
        -- model and field references. Missing-index errors show the required
        -- extractor or explain why the relationship shape is unsupported.
        -- Corrections are available in both the message and structured error
        -- data; calculation suggestions disclose candidate-dependent work.
        -- Plain field orders never fall back to population-result scanning.
        -- If current membership prevents the supported indexed execution, fail
        -- explicitly instead. An explicit call expression opts into calculation.
        -- Calculation arguments are resolved eagerly; all declared arguments
        -- may be read for every candidate, even if the function could ignore an
        -- argument for some inputs. This cost is measured, not assumed away.
        -- A typed oversized-storage-key failure propagates to the caller. It
        -- does not trigger another unindexed replay or a population scan.

    @invariant IsolationAndFreshness
        -- Every relationship uses the trusted context's event store, cache and
        -- tenant. The application supplies no separate event-history identifier.
        -- No definition supplies tenant routing. Dependencies use the existing
        -- keyed read-session freshness and snapshot guarantees; this API
        -- does not strengthen them into event-log transactions across folds.
        -- Relevant model dependencies must be available to query subscription
        -- wiring. Time-dependent calculations use the explicit request date.

    @invariant HonestPerformance
        -- Planning must expose population or candidate-dependent work for
        -- inspection. Unsupported bounded execution is not silently described
        -- as indexed pagination. Moving an existing scan into Grain does not
        -- eliminate population-dependent work.
        -- Validate against canonical composition and measure complete query plus
        -- rendering, allocation, selectivity, page depth and concurrency.

    @guidance
        -- Scope is one root, to-one relationships, literal search,
        -- declared orders and explicit pure
        -- calculations. No general SQL language or collection aggregation API
        -- is proposed. Existing low-level keyed APIs remain available.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A registered read model: a named reducer plus the event types it folds and a
-- cache version. Registration is by qualified name; bumping the version
-- invalidates all cached state for that read model (it is part of the cache
-- identity). Partition functions are present only for partitioned read models.
entity ReadModel {
    -- The qualified name a projection is requested by. Together with the version
    -- it forms the read model's cache identity.
    name: String
    -- The event types this read model subscribes to. The engine folds exactly
    -- the matching events.
    event_types: Set<String>
    -- Bump to invalidate previously cached state.
    version: Integer
    state_schema: StateSchema?
    -- Present only for partitioned read models: when true the read model shards
    -- its state by a partition key derived from entity state. When false a single
    -- monolithic-or-segmented projection is kept.
    is_partitioned: Boolean
    -- How long in-process state may be served before re-checking the store.
    l1_ttl: Duration

    -- The cached projections derived from this read model.
    caches: Cache with read_model = this
}

-- The engine's cached projection for one (read model, tenant, scope, partition)
-- combination. It is a memo of the fold: the accumulated state plus the
-- watermark up to which it was folded. A read advances it by folding the events
-- after its watermark; it is never authoritative over the event stream, which
-- can always re-derive it from scratch.
entity Cache {
    read_model: ReadModel
    -- The tenant whose events this projection folds. Part of the cache identity:
    -- no two tenants ever share a cache entry.
    tenant: Tenant
    -- The scope that narrowed the stream, if any. Part of the cache identity.
    scope: Scope?
    -- For a partitioned read model, which partition this entry holds. Absent for
    -- a full or unpartitioned projection. Part of the cache identity.
    partition_key: PartitionKey?
    -- The accumulated projection state.
    state: Any
    -- The identifiers of the domain entities held in this cached state. For a
    -- partitioned read model this is the entity set of one partition; the engine
    -- routes each event to a partition by the entity it concerns and detects a
    -- move when an entity's partition key changes.
    entity_ids: Set<EntityId>
    -- The id of the last event folded into state. The next read resumes strictly
    -- after this id. Absent only before the first event is folded.
    watermark: EventId?
    -- When the in-process copy was last confirmed against the event store (folded
    -- or revalidated). The freshness window is measured from here.
    validated_at: Timestamp
    -- Whether this entry was last served only after catching up against the
    -- store, rather than from the stale fast path. Always true for a zero-TTL
    -- read model, which never takes the fast path.
    served_after_catch_up: Boolean
    -- Whether a matching event has been committed since this entry was last
    -- folded, so its state lags the stream. A stale entry is not eagerly
    -- recomputed; the next read that does not take the fast path catches it up.
    stale: Boolean

    -- The entry's state reflects the stream exactly when it is not stale.
    is_current: not stale
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types (partition keys and ids)
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The key identifying a partition of a partitioned read model's state. Opaque to
-- the engine; derived from entity state by the read model's partition function.
value PartitionKey {
    descriptor: String
}

-- The identifier of a domain entity within a read model's state map, extracted
-- from an event. Used to route events to partitions and to detect moves.
value EntityId {
    value: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A caller requests the current projection of a read model for a tenant, with an
-- optional scope and (for partitioned models) an optional partition key. The
-- engine returns the projection, caching it. Whether it folds from scratch,
-- resumes from the durable tier, or returns the in-process copy is an
-- optimisation: the value returned is the projection over the matching events.
rule ProjectReadModel {
    when: ProjectRequested(read_model, tenant, scope?, partition_key?)

    let cache = Cache{read_model: read_model, tenant: tenant, scope: scope, partition_key: partition_key}
    let known_watermark = if exists cache: cache.watermark else: null
    let fresh =
        exists cache
        and read_model.l1_ttl > 0.seconds
        and now - cache.validated_at < read_model.l1_ttl

    ensures:
        if fresh:
            -- Within the in-process freshness window: serve the cached state
            -- without touching the event store. No catch-up occurred.
            cache.served_after_catch_up = false
            ProjectionServed(tenant: tenant, state: cache.state)
        else:
            -- Otherwise catch up: fold the events past the known watermark onto
            -- the cached state (the whole stream when there is no cache yet).
            let new_events =
                if exists cache:
                    read_after(tenant, effective_query(read_model, scope), known_watermark)
                else:
                    read(tenant, effective_query(read_model, scope))
            let folded = fold_events(read_model, current_state(cache), new_events)

            -- The projection now reflects every matching event; the watermark
            -- advances to the last one folded (or stays put when none arrived).
            Cache.created(
                read_model: read_model,
                tenant: tenant,
                scope: scope,
                partition_key: partition_key,
                state: folded.state,
                watermark: folded.watermark,
                validated_at: now,
                served_after_catch_up: true,
                stale: false
            )
            ProjectionServed(tenant: tenant, state: folded.state)

    @guidance
        -- The two tiers and the size-transparent / partitioned storage layouts
        -- are all variations on one idea: memoise the fold and resume from a
        -- watermark. The Cache entity is that memo regardless of where it
        -- physically lives. read_after over the matching query is what makes
        -- catch-up gapless and reprocessing-free; the event store contract
        -- (EventStream.TenantScopedAscending) is what makes read_after correct.
        --
        -- For a partitioned read model the per-partition entries are independent
        -- memos keyed by partition_key; a cross-partition move is observed as the
        -- moved entity being shed from one partition's state and adopted into
        -- another's during the fold (see contract Partitioning.CrossPartitionMove).
        --
        -- `validated_at` and the freshness window are an in-process concept; with
        -- l1_ttl = 0 the `fresh` branch is never taken and every read re-checks
        -- the store, so a read is never stale (invariant FreshWhenZeroTtl).
}

-- A new fact in a tenant's stream makes any cached projection that subscribes to
-- the event's type lag the stream: its watermark now precedes the event. The
-- engine does not recompute eagerly — it marks the entry stale, and the next
-- read that is not served from the freshness window folds the new event in.
rule EventCommittedStalesCaches {
    when: event: Event.created
    ensures:
        for c in Caches where tenant = event.tenant and event.is_subscribed_by(read_model):
            c.stale = true

    @guidance
        -- This is the truthful, pull-based staleness relationship, not an eager
        -- projection: it explains why a zero-TTL read re-checks the store on every
        -- request (it must, or it could serve a stale entry) and why a positive
        -- TTL may serve a stale entry for the duration of the window. The
        -- subscription guard means an event of a type the read model ignores
        -- never stales its caches — it would not have been folded anyway.
}

-- A tenant the engine has not seen before starts with no cached projections, so
-- its first projection request is necessarily a from-scratch fold. The cache set
-- is built lazily on demand, never provisioned ahead of a read.
rule TenantFirstSeen {
    when: tenant: Tenant.created
    ensures: not exists Cache{tenant: tenant}
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- RM7: a cache entry belongs to exactly one tenant. No two tenants ever share an
-- entry, so one tenant's events can never leak into another's projection — even
-- when the same read model, scope and partition are read for both.
-- Distinct identities remain isolated when their ordinary value hashes collide,
-- in both resident and persisted caches.
invariant CachePerTenant {
    for a in Caches:
        for b in Caches:
            (a != b
             and a.read_model = b.read_model
             and a.scope = b.scope
             and a.partition_key = b.partition_key)
                implies a.tenant != b.tenant
}

-- RM8: distinct scopes of the same read model and tenant are distinct cache
-- entries. A scoped projection and the unscoped one (or two differently scoped
-- ones) never share accumulated state.
-- Collection iteration order is not scope identity: equal maps and sets retain
-- the same cache identity regardless of their construction order. Partition
-- identities follow the same rules.
invariant CachePerScope {
    for a in Caches:
        for b in Caches:
            (a != b
             and a.read_model = b.read_model
             and a.tenant = b.tenant
             and a.partition_key = b.partition_key)
                implies a.scope != b.scope
}

-- RM10: for a partitioned read model, an entity belongs to at most one partition
-- at a time. Two cache entries for the same read model, tenant and scope are
-- different partitions, and their entity sets are disjoint — no entity id is
-- held by two partitions at once, which is exactly what a cross-partition move
-- preserves.
invariant EntityInOnePartition {
    for a in Caches:
        for b in Caches:
            (a != b
             and a.read_model = b.read_model
             and a.tenant = b.tenant
             and a.scope = b.scope)
                implies
                    a.partition_key != b.partition_key
                    and not a.entity_ids.any(e => e in b.entity_ids)
}

-- A cache's watermark, once set, is the id of a real event that the read model
-- subscribes to in the same tenant's stream. The watermark is therefore the
-- fold cursor over the matching events, never an id from another tenant or an
-- event type the read model ignores.
invariant WatermarkNamesAFoldedEvent {
    for c in Caches:
        c.watermark != null implies
            Events.any(e =>
                e.id = c.watermark
                and e.tenant = c.tenant
                and e.event_type in c.read_model.event_types)
}

-- Every cache belongs to a tenant the engine knows; a cache cannot exist for a
-- tenant outside the set the store admits. This pins the projection's scope to
-- a real tenant.
invariant CacheTenantIsKnown {
    for c in Caches:
        Tenants.any(t => t = c.tenant)
}

-- RM6: with the freshness window disabled (l1_ttl = 0), every read re-checks the
-- event store, so no cache can serve state that omits an already-committed
-- matching event. Stated as a lower bound on freshness: a zero-TTL read model's
-- cache is only ever served after catching up against the store, never from the
-- stale fast path. (A matching commit still marks such an entry stale until the
-- next read folds it in — see EventCommittedStalesCaches — so currency is restored
-- on the next read rather than held continuously; that catch-up-then-current
-- sequence is a temporal property, not a single-point-in-time assertion.)
invariant FreshWhenZeroTtl {
    for c in Caches:
        c.read_model.l1_ttl = 0.seconds implies c.served_after_catch_up
}

-- A read model is identified by its name and version together; that pair is its
-- cache identity. Two distinct registered read models never collide on both, so
-- a version bump yields a fresh identity whose caches are necessarily empty
-- until refolded.
invariant ReadModelIdentity {
    for a in ReadModels:
        for b in ReadModels:
            a != b implies (a.name != b.name or a.version != b.version)
}

-- A cache entry's shape follows its read model: only a partitioned read model's
-- entries name a partition, and an unpartitioned read model's entries never do.
-- This pins the partition_key field's presence to the read model's kind, so the
-- per-partition isolation guarantee only ever applies where partitioning is in
-- effect. Stated over each read model's own cache set, the relation that defines
-- which entries a version bump orphans.
invariant CacheShapeFollowsReadModel {
    for rm in ReadModels:
        rm.caches.all(c => rm.is_partitioned implies c.partition_key != null)
        and rm.caches.all(c => not rm.is_partitioned implies c.partition_key = null)
}

-- A projection just served after catching up against the store is current: it
-- reflects every matching event committed up to that read. Currency is the
-- absence of staleness (Cache.is_current), and a catch-up clears staleness, so
-- the freshly served entry is not lagging the stream at the moment it is read.
invariant CaughtUpEntriesAreCurrent {
    for c in Caches:
        c.served_after_catch_up implies c.is_current
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The party on the other side of this boundary is code, not a person: a query
-- handler asking for a projection, and the application code that defines and
-- registers read models. Both are tenant-scoped, so the surfaces face the
-- Tenant the caller operates on.

-- The registration boundary: how application code declares a read model the
-- engine can later project.
surface ReadModelRegistration {
    facing tenant: Tenant

    exposes:
        -- The set of read models the engine knows how to project.
        ReadModels

    provides:
        RegisterReadModel(tenant, read_model)

    @guarantee VersionInvalidatesCache
        -- A read model's version is part of its cache identity. Bumping it makes
        -- previously cached state unreachable, so the next read re-folds from
        -- scratch — the supported way to evolve a reducer.
}

-- The projection boundary: how a tenant-scoped caller obtains the current state
-- of a read model.
surface ReadModelProjection {
    facing tenant: Tenant

    provides:
        ProjectRequested(read_model, tenant, scope?, partition_key?)

    contracts:
        demands EventStream
        demands Reducer
        demands StateCodec
        fulfils Projection
        fulfils Cache
        fulfils Partitioning

    @guarantee CacheIsTransparent
        -- The state returned is the reducer folded over the matching event
        -- stream for this tenant, whichever tier answered. Caching changes
        -- latency and cost, never the value.

    @guarantee TenantIsolation
        -- RM7: a projection folds and returns only the requesting tenant's
        -- events; cache entries are keyed by tenant and never shared across
        -- tenants.

    @guarantee ScopeIsolation
        -- RM8: a scoped projection reflects only the events its scope selects,
        -- and is cached separately from the unscoped projection and from other
        -- scopes.

    @guarantee ProbeIsResilient
        -- To route a not-yet-seen event to a partition the engine probes the
        -- reducer against empty state. A reducer that assumes prior state may
        -- fail that probe; the engine treats a failed probe as "not relevant"
        -- and the read still completes correctly.
}

surface KeyedFoldBoundary {
    contracts:
        demands KeyedFoldDefinition
        fulfils KeyedFoldMaterialization
        fulfils KeyedFoldReads
        fulfils ComposedReadQuery

    @guarantee OneProjectionLifecycle
        -- Application code supplies independent pure folds and optional result
        -- functions. It does not maintain transactions, cache writes, checkpoints,
        -- result invalidation, or a second summary-refresh lifecycle.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "A read model's reducer can evolve only by bumping its version, which orphans the old cached state. Should the engine model reclamation of orphaned cache entries, or is that left to operational tooling?"
open question "The durable write-back is deferred until write_back_threshold new events, so the durable tier can lag the in-process tier. Is the only consequence a colder cold-read (re-fold the remainder), or are there durability expectations on the cache worth stating explicitly?"
open question "Cross-partition move detection relies on the reducer's partition function being stable for an unchanged entity. Should the spec state a contract that partition_of is deterministic and depends only on entity state, beyond the prose in contract Partitioning?"

open question "Computed ordering: what bounded execution can support exact request-dependent calculated orders without a population scan or application-maintained state? The query surface alone does not resolve this."
-- Query model dependencies are exposed as the existing model/version subscription
-- map. Applications retain their endpoint declarations and time-based refresh
-- keys; this API introduces no automatic clock scheduling or endpoint macro.
